+// The leading segment of the tab strip's combo button, showing the Project
+// (== Chromium Profile, see the "Projects" plan) this window belongs to as
+// its avatar plus its name. This is a real per-window identity -- always
+// visible, unlike the location-bar AppSwap Profile/Route Group chips, which
+// only show up on relevant pages.
+//
+// A TabStripFlatEdgeButton rather than a chip of its own, so it joins the
+// tab-groups and tab-search buttons into one group of three instead of
+// floating beside them: TabStripComboButton::SetLeadingButton() adopts it and
+// gives all three their flat edges. It is built by whoever builds the tab
+// strip region (see HorizontalTabStripRegionView and
+// VerticalTabStripRegionView) rather than by the combo button, because the
+// profile menu below lives in //chrome/browser/ui, which depends on the combo
+// button's own target and so cannot be depended on from it.
+//
+// It carries only the avatar when the combo button is laid out vertically,
+// which is what the vertical tab strip does while collapsed -- there is no
+// room for a name in a strip that narrow, and the avatar alone still says
+// which project this window is.
+//
+// Clicking it reuses Chromium's own native profile-switcher bubble
+// (BrowserWindowFeatures::profile_menu_coordinator()/ProfileMenuView)
+// instead of a custom dropdown: this button claims the
+// kToolbarAvatarButtonElementId element identifier that
+// ProfileMenuCoordinator anchors its bubble to. The real AvatarToolbarButton
+// is hidden in this build (see ToolbarView's constructor, which clears the
+// identifier off of it so there's no conflict) -- so
+// chrome::ShowAvatarMenu() shows the exact same bubble the stock avatar
+// button would, anchored to this button instead. That gets every feature of
+// that bubble (switch/create/delete project, guest mode, DICE sign-in) for
+// free, correctly retextured to "project" wording via the string changes in
+// profiles_strings.grdp/chromium_strings.grd/their .xtb translations, with
+// no custom menu code to maintain here.
+//
+// This is why this button's two files are compiled into the main
+// //chrome/browser/ui target (see chrome/browser/ui/BUILD.gn) rather than
+// the separate //chrome/browser/ui/views/app_swap target the other AppSwap
+// tab-strip/location-bar buttons live in: chrome::ShowAvatarMenu() and
+// ProfileMenuCoordinator are only reachable from within that target, which
+// depends on (not from) app_swap, so pulling this button in the other
+// direction would be circular.
